ARM_FILES = {'evidc': {'agent.yaml': "# evidc (r76): evid3's evidence stage made cheap (thinking off, 8 s script, last 500 commits) + combo1 as the fixer (scripts/make_plus_arms.py)\nname: swe_agent\nagent_class: SequentialAgent\nsub_agents:\n  - config_path: evidence.yaml\n  - config_path: fixer.yaml\n", 'configs/evidence_sampling.yaml': '# r76 cheap evidence stage: thinking off; a mechanical job (pick names, call the script once, copy lines).\n# In the installed harness (adk_submission 0.2.13, resolvers/generation.py) any thinking_budget > 0 turns thinking ON and a\n# missing budget is filled with 4096, so include_thoughts: false alone does not switch it off; thinking_budget: 0 does\n# (schema ge=0, limits 0-32768; the request then carries enable_thinking false and no thinking_token_budget).\ntemperature: 0.2\ntop_p: 0.95\nmax_output_tokens: 1024\nthinking_config:\n  thinking_budget: 0\n  include_thoughts: false\n', 'configs/sampling.yaml': 'temperature: 0.2\ntop_p: 0.95\nmax_output_tokens: 8192\nthinking_config:\n  thinking_budget: 4096\n  include_thoughts: true\n', 'eval_config.yaml': 'evaluation:\n  timeout_seconds: 240\n  max_tool_calls: 29\n  max_time_minutes: 8\n  max_turns: 80\n', 'evidence.yaml': 'name: evidence\nmodel: gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct\ndescription: Runs one repository search for names in the issue and copies the lines that matter.\ninstruction: !include prompts/evidence.md\ninclude_contents: none  # fresh: the issue is in its system prompt (the harness re-runs the root agent after a nudge)\ntools:\n  - get_status\nskills:\n  - skills/evidence\noutput_key: evidence\ngenerate_content_config: !include configs/evidence_sampling.yaml\n', 'fixer.yaml': "name: fixer\nmodel: gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct\ninstruction: !include prompts/fixer.md\ntools:  # combo1's tool list\n  - run_command\n  - read_file\n  - edit_file\n  - write_file\n  - get_status\n  - submit_patch\ngenerate_content_config: !include configs/sampling.yaml\n", 'prompts/evidence.md': "Your only job is one search of the repository for the agent who fixes the issue after you. Your reply is copied into that agent's instructions and stays in front of it for the whole task, so it must hold real lines from the repository, not your opinions. You will not read code and you will not work on the fix.\n\nChoose two to four exact names from the issue below that the fix most likely touches: functions, methods, classes, options or parameters, written exactly as they appear in the issue or as they would appear in the code. Prefer the most specific names, such as convert_underscores, over common words, such as Header.\n\nCall run_skill_script with skill_name evidence, file_path scripts/evidence.py and positional_args holding those names, one name per item. That is your only call.\n\nThen reply with the lines of its result that best show where the fix belongs and how this code is meant to behave: where the names are defined, tests that use them, and the lines an earlier commit changed around them. Copy each line exactly as it was printed, with its file:line or commit, at most 10 lines and nothing else. If nothing in the result bears on the issue, reply with the single line NOTHING RELEVANT.\n\n# THE ISSUE (verbatim)\n{problem_description}", 'prompts/fixer.md': 'You are an autonomous software engineer. Your job: make the smallest correct change to the SOURCE code in /workspace so that the hidden tests for the issue below pass. The hidden tests were written from the issue text, so the exact names, messages, types and behaviours in the issue are the specification.\n\n# HARD LIMITS\n- 28 tool calls and 8 minutes in total. When either runs out, the working tree is graded as it is. An edit that exists counts, even if you never call `submit_patch`. An unedited tree always scores zero.\n- Never create, modify or delete test files (`test_*.py`, `*_test.py`, anything under `tests/`, `pytest.ini`, `conftest.py`). This is an automatic failure.\n- Scratch files go in /tmp only, and only through run_command (for example `cat > /tmp/check.py <<\'EOF\'`). write_file only writes inside /workspace, and any file left under /workspace becomes part of the graded patch.\n- Never run a full test suite (bare `pytest`, `pytest .`, `unittest discover`). Always name one test file, and add `-k` when you can.\n- Never look outside /workspace. All dependencies are already installed.\n\n# CALL PLAN\n- Calls 1-8: locate and read. Calls 9-12: edit. You MUST have edited a source file by call 12. If you are unsure, make your best-guess edit in the most likely place now. A wrong edit can still be corrected. Pure reading cannot score.\n- Calls 13-20: verify and correct.\n- Call 20 onward: no new exploration. Check, fix, submit.\n- Always finish with `git status --short && git diff` followed by `submit_patch`.\n\n# PROCEDURE\n\n## 1. Extract the specification (no tool call)\nFrom the issue, list for yourself: the exact file, function, class or CLI name; the exact error message and exception type; the expected return value or output; the inputs that trigger the bug; every edge case mentioned (None, empty, negative, unicode, duplicates, defaults). Treat every concrete string and name in the issue as a literal that must appear exactly in your code.\n\n## 2. Locate\n- Use exact paths from the issue directly. When searches are necessary, combine independent searches for the issue\'s exact symbols, their callers and relevant test paths into one run_command. Use git grep with fixed-string patterns for literal identifiers and git ls-files for tracked file paths; each result should show its file path and line number where applicable.\n- Batch only searches whose inputs are already known. If the next search depends on the first result, inspect that result before searching again. Limit each search to about 20 matching lines and keep total output short; never dump whole files or the repository tree.\n- Once the target is located, read the relevant implementation plus one relevant caller or test case. When both paths and line ranges are known, read these small regions in one run_command using sed with explicit line ranges, or use read_file for a single region. Aim for about 40 lines per region and at most three regions per command. Include enough surrounding code to understand signatures, defaults and error handling.\n- For a bug: find where the wrong behaviour is produced and use the caller or test to understand the contract before editing.\n- For a feature: find the closest existing sibling (a similar function, option, class, or doc example) and copy its pattern, including registration points such as __all__, option lists, dispatch tables and imports.\n- Reuse paths and test names already discovered. Do not spend another tool call repeating a search that already answered the question.\n- If the issue includes a code snippet that reproduces the problem, you may run it once with python -c (one call, never create a file) to see the real error.\n\n## 3. Edit\n- Use edit_file for changes to existing code. Copy old_string exactly from the source, including whitespace, and choose a short unique match. Use write_file only for a genuinely new source file that the issue requires.\n- If a tool result says "mandatory input parameters are not present", your edit_file or write_file call was cut apart: a text ended with a backtick or a stray quote and swallowed the next argument. Do not send the same call again. End every text right after its last character, never with a backtick. If it fails a second time, use the heredoc script described next.\n- If edit_file reports no match or multiple matches, read the exact source region and retry with a smaller unique snippet. Never repeat an identical failed edit. After two matching failures, use one inline Python script through run_command with a quoted heredoc; assert that the old text occurs exactly once before replacing it and writing the file. Preserve unrelated content and do not rewrite the file from memory.\n- Fix the cause, not the symptom. Do not special-case the example from the issue.\n- Change only what the issue requires. Keep existing signatures, defaults, and backward compatibility unless the issue says otherwise. Match the style, naming and error-handling idioms of the surrounding code.\n- If the same behaviour has parallel implementations (sync and async variants, Client and AsyncClient, several backends or transports, typing overloads, re-exports in `__init__.py` or `__all__`), apply the change to every one of them. Hidden tests often exercise more than one.\n- Use the exact exception class and exact message text from the issue. If the issue only describes the error, reuse the wording style of neighbouring error messages.\n- Handle every edge case you listed in step 1.\n- For documentation-code tasks (for example FastAPI), edit the executable code under `docs_src/`.\n\n## 4. Verify (at most 2 calls)\n- Find the test file with `find tests -name "*keyword*.py"`, then run `timeout 150 python -m pytest tests/path/test_x.py -x -q -k keyword`. Use `python3 -m unittest tests.test_x` for unittest projects.\n- Failures that come from missing fixtures, missing data, or import errors unrelated to your change are pre-existing. Ignore them. Never repair tests or the environment.\n- If a test related to your change fails, read the traceback, fix the source once, and rerun once. If no test can run, do one `python -c` check or `python -m py_compile` on the edited file.\n\n## 5. Final check and submit\n- Run `git status --short && git diff` in one call. `git diff` alone hides untracked files, but `submit_patch` includes them. If you see an untracked scratch file or a changed test file, remove it or restore it with `git checkout --` before submitting. Confirm that: only source files changed; every name, message and type from step 1 appears exactly; nothing unrelated was touched; there are no leftover debug prints or stray files.\n- Call `submit_patch`. Confirm `patch_size > 0` and `files_changed > 0`. If either is 0, you have not edited anything. Edit now and submit again.\n- End with a single-sentence summary of the fix.\n\n# DECISION RULES\n- Ambiguous issue: choose the most literal reading of the issue text and act on it. Do not ask questions and do not stall.\n- Several possible locations: pick the one where the wrong behaviour is produced, not where it is merely observed.\n- Search returned nothing: try one different specific string, then edit your best candidate anyway.\n- Stuck after a failed fix: make a different, smaller change. Do not repeat the same edit.\n- Never conclude that nothing needs to change. Every task requires a non-empty source patch.\n\n# REPOSITORY EVIDENCE (found before you started; it stays here for the whole task)\nLines copied from a search of this repository for names in the issue: where they are defined, tests that use them, and lines that earlier commits changed around them. Use them to find and understand the code; the issue stays the specification.\n{evidence?}\n', 'skills/evidence/SKILL.md': "---\nname: evidence\ndescription: Searches the repository's tests, definitions, git history and changelog for a few exact names from the issue. Call run_skill_script with skill_name evidence, file_path scripts/evidence.py and positional_args holding the names.\n---\n\nCall run_skill_script with skill_name evidence, file_path scripts/evidence.py and positional_args holding two to four exact\nnames from the issue, one name per item. It prints what the repository says about each name and takes a few seconds.\n", 'skills/evidence/scripts/evidence.py': '"""Repository evidence for a few exact names from the issue, in one call (arm evid1, scripts/make_mine_arms.py).\n\nRun once per task by the evidence agent through run_skill_script (skill "evidence"), names as positional_args:\n\n    run_skill_script skill_name=evidence file_path=scripts/evidence.py positional_args=["convert_underscores", "Header"]\n\nWhy: r60\'s scouts found good evidence (the right test files, the earlier commits on the buggy lines) but explored for\n2-4 minutes. This script does the same fixed searches in seconds, read-only, inside the repository:\n  - tests that mention the name (git grep in test files),\n  - where the name is defined (def / class lines),\n  - the most recent commits that added or removed the name (git log -S) and the changed lines around it in the newest one,\n  - changelog lines that mention it.\nEvery git call has its own timeout and the whole script stops adding sections after DEADLINE seconds.\n"""\nimport inspect\nimport os\nimport re\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport time\n\nONCE = True  # evid2 (r64): True -> a second call is refused (r63: told to make one call, the agent made 2-17)\nDONE_FILE = os.path.join(".git", "agent", "evidence_done")  # holds the first result, shown again on a refused call\nREPLY = \'Now reply with at most 10 lines of this result, copied exactly, and stop.\'  # evid3 (r65) swaps this: the stage\'s reply becomes its output\nDEADLINE = 8  # r76: seconds for all sections (evid3: 40; large private repos made it the stage\'s main cost)\nCALL_TIMEOUT = 4  # seconds per git call (evid3: 12)\nMAX_NAMES = 5\nOUT_MAX = 4800  # characters; the harness shows about 5000\nTEST_LINES = 6\nDEF_LINES = 3\nCOMMITS = 5\nHUNK_TRIES = 5  # changed lines come from the newest of the first HUNK_TRIES commits that has some\nHUNK_LINES = 18\nWINDOW = 3  # context lines around each changed line that has the name\nLOG_LINES = 3\nLINE_MAX = 150\nTEST_PATHS = [":(glob)**/test/**/*.py", ":(glob)**/tests/**/*.py", ":(glob)**/testing/**/*.py", ":(glob)**/test_*.py", ":(glob)**/*_test.py"]\nPY = [":(glob)**/*.py"]  # history and changed lines: Python files only (docs translations drown everything else)\nLOG_PATHS = [":(glob)**/CHANGELOG*", ":(glob)**/CHANGES*", ":(glob)**/HISTORY*", ":(glob)**/NEWS*", ":(glob)**/release-notes*",\n             ":(glob)**/changelog*"]\n\n\ndef workspace():\n    """The repository root. The skill wrapper chdirs into a temp dir, so look for the directory it started in."""\n    for frame in inspect.stack():\n        cwd = frame.frame.f_locals.get("_orig_cwd")\n        if isinstance(cwd, str) and os.path.isdir(os.path.join(cwd, ".git")):\n            return cwd\n    for cand in (os.environ.get("PYTHONPATH", "").split(os.pathsep)[0], "/workspace", os.getcwd()):\n        if cand and os.path.isdir(os.path.join(cand, ".git")):\n            return cand\n    sys.exit("repository not found")\n\n\ndef git(ws, args, start):\n    """stdout lines of one git call, [] on error, timeout or when the deadline has passed."""\n    left = DEADLINE - (time.time() - start)\n    if left < 1:\n        return None\n    try:\n        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-c", "core.quotepath=off"] + args, cwd=ws, capture_output=True, text=True,\n                           timeout=min(CALL_TIMEOUT, left), stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, errors="replace")\n    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:\n        return []\n    return r.stdout.splitlines() if r.returncode in (0, 1) else []\n\n\ndef short(line):\n    line = line.rstrip()\n    return line if len(line) <= LINE_MAX else line[:LINE_MAX] + " ..."\n\n\ndef names_from_args():\n    raw = [a for a in sys.argv[1:] if a != "--"]\n    names = []\n    for a in raw:\n        for part in re.split(r"[\\n,]", a):\n            n = part.strip().strip("`\'\\"").strip()\n            if len(n) >= 3 and n not in names:\n                names.append(n)\n    return names[:MAX_NAMES]\n\n\ndef newest_hunks(ws, commit, name, start):\n    """Changed lines that contain NAME in COMMIT (Python source, not tests), with WINDOW lines around each, at most\n    HUNK_LINES. Files the commit created or deleted are skipped: a whole new file says nothing about the change."""\n    lines = git(ws, ["show", "--format=", "-U3", "--no-color", commit, "--"] + PY\n                + [":(exclude,glob)" + p[len(":(glob)"):] for p in TEST_PATHS], start)\n    if not lines:\n        return []\n    out, cur_file, hunk, skip = [], None, [], False\n\n    def flush():\n        if skip or not hunk:\n            return\n        hits = [i for i, h in enumerate(hunk) if h[:1] in "+-" and name in h]\n        keep = sorted({j for i in hits for j in range(max(0, i - WINDOW), min(len(hunk), i + WINDOW + 1))})\n        if keep:\n            out.append(f"  {cur_file}:")\n            prev = None\n            for j in keep:\n                if prev is not None and j != prev + 1:\n                    out.append("    ...")\n                out.append("    " + short(hunk[j]))\n                prev = j\n\n    for ln in lines:\n        if ln.startswith("diff --git"):\n            flush()\n            hunk, skip, cur_file = [], False, None\n        elif ln.startswith(("new file", "deleted file")):\n            skip = True\n        elif ln.startswith("+++ "):\n            cur_file = ln[6:] if ln.startswith("+++ b/") else ln[4:]\n        elif ln.startswith(("index ", "--- ", "similarity", "rename ")):\n            continue\n        elif ln.startswith("@@"):\n            flush()\n            hunk = []\n        else:\n            hunk.append(ln)\n    flush()\n    return out[:HUNK_LINES]\n\n\n_RANGE = []\n\n\ndef recent(ws, start):\n    """r76: git log -S only over the last 500 commits (the whole history is slow on large repositories)."""\n    if not _RANGE:\n        revs = git(ws, ["rev-list", "--max-count=500", "HEAD"], start) or []\n        _RANGE.append([revs[-1] + "..HEAD"] if len(revs) == 500 else [])\n    return _RANGE[0]\n\n\ndef section(ws, name, start):\n    out = [f"== {name}"]\n    tests = git(ws, ["grep", "-n", "-I", "-F", "-e", name, "--"] + TEST_PATHS, start)\n    if tests is None:\n        return out + ["  (skipped: time)"]\n    out.append(f"tests mentioning it: {len(tests)} lines" + ("" if tests else " (none)"))\n    out.extend("  " + short(t) for t in tests[:TEST_LINES])\n    if re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*", name):\n        defs = git(ws, ["grep", "-n", "-I", "-E", "-e", rf"^[[:space:]]*(async def|def|class) {name}([^A-Za-z0-9_]|$)", "--", "*.py"], start) or []\n        if defs:\n            out.append("defined at:")\n            out.extend("  " + short(d) for d in defs[:DEF_LINES])\n    log = git(ws, ["log", "-S", name, "--no-merges", "--format=%h %ad %s", "--date=short", f"-n{COMMITS}"] + recent(ws, start) + ["--"] + PY, start)\n    if log is None:\n        return out + ["  (history skipped: time)"]\n    if log:\n        out.append("commits that added or removed it (newest first):")\n        out.extend("  " + short(c) for c in log)\n        for c in log[:HUNK_TRIES]:\n            hunks = newest_hunks(ws, c.split()[0], name, start)\n            if hunks:\n                out.append(f"what {c.split()[0]} changed around it:")\n                out.extend(hunks)\n                break\n    else:\n        out.append("commits that added or removed it: none found")\n    notes = git(ws, ["grep", "-n", "-I", "-i", "-F", "-e", name, "--"] + LOG_PATHS, start) or []\n    if notes:\n        out.append("changelog:")\n        out.extend("  " + short(n) for n in notes[:LOG_LINES])\n    return out\n\n\ndef main():\n    start = time.time()\n    names = names_from_args()\n    if not names:\n        print("NO NAMES: pass 2 to 5 exact names from the issue as positional_args, for example "\n              "[\\"function_name\\", \\"ClassName\\", \\"option_name\\"].")\n        return\n    ws = workspace()\n    done = os.path.join(ws, DONE_FILE)\n    if ONCE and os.path.exists(done):  # also after the harness re-runs the stage with no history: show the result again\n        print("ALREADY DONE: the search already ran and a second call is refused. Its result was:\\n"\n              + open(done, errors="replace").read() + "\\n" + REPLY)\n        return\n    parts = [f"REPOSITORY EVIDENCE for {\', \'.join(names)} (read-only searches; the issue stays the specification)"]\n    for n in names:\n        parts.extend(section(ws, n, start))\n    text = "\\n".join(parts)\n    if len(text) > OUT_MAX:\n        text = text[:OUT_MAX] + "\\n[cut]"\n    if ONCE:\n        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(done), exist_ok=True)\n        open(done, "w").write(text)\n        text += "\\nThis was the only search: a second call is refused. " + REPLY\n    print(text + f"\\n[done in {time.time() - start:.0f} s]")\n\n\nif __name__ == "__main__":\n    main()\n'}, 'evpipe': {'agent.yaml': '# evpipe (r92): evidence -> locator (look skill, refused after 6 calls) -> three tool-less proposers in parallel -> applier (scripts/make_r92_arms.py)\nname: swe_agent\nagent_class: SequentialAgent\nsub_agents:\n  - config_path: evidence.yaml\n  - config_path: locator.yaml\n  - config_path: proposers.yaml\n  - config_path: applier.yaml\n', 'applier.yaml': 'name: applier\nmodel: gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct\ndescription: "Applies the best proposal, checks it once and submits."\ninstruction: !include prompts/applier.md\ninclude_contents: none\ntools:\n  - run_command\n  - read_file\n  - edit_file\n  - write_file\n  - get_status\n  - submit_patch\ngenerate_content_config: !include configs/sampling.yaml\n', 'configs/evidence_sampling.yaml': '# r76 cheap evidence stage: thinking off; a mechanical job (pick names, call the script once, copy lines).\n# In the installed harness (adk_submission 0.2.13, resolvers/generation.py) any thinking_budget > 0 turns thinking ON and a\n# missing budget is filled with 4096, so include_thoughts: false alone does not switch it off; thinking_budget: 0 does\n# (schema ge=0, limits 0-32768; the request then carries enable_thinking false and no thinking_token_budget).\ntemperature: 0.2\ntop_p: 0.95\nmax_output_tokens: 1024\nthinking_config:\n  thinking_budget: 0\n  include_thoughts: false\n', 'configs/prop_a_sampling.yaml': '# r92 evpipe proposer a\ntemperature: 0.2\ntop_p: 0.95\nmax_output_tokens: 8192\nthinking_config:\n  thinking_budget: 4096\n  include_thoughts: true\n', 'configs/prop_b_sampling.yaml': '# r92 evpipe proposer b\ntemperature: 0.7\ntop_p: 0.95\nmax_output_tokens: 8192\nthinking_config:\n  thinking_budget: 4096\n  include_thoughts: true\n', 'configs/prop_c_sampling.yaml': '# r92 evpipe proposer c\ntemperature: 1.0\ntop_p: 0.95\ntop_k: 64\nmax_output_tokens: 8192\nthinking_config:\n  thinking_budget: 4096\n  include_thoughts: true\n', 'configs/sampling.yaml': 'temperature: 0.2\ntop_p: 0.95\nmax_output_tokens: 8192\nthinking_config:\n  thinking_budget: 4096\n  include_thoughts: true\n', 'eval_config.yaml': 'evaluation:\n  timeout_seconds: 240\n  max_tool_calls: 29\n  max_time_minutes: 8\n  max_turns: 80\n', 'evidence.yaml': 'name: evidence\nmodel: gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct\ndescription: Runs one repository search for names in the issue and copies the lines that matter.\ninstruction: !include prompts/evidence.md\ninclude_contents: none  # fresh: the issue is in its system prompt (the harness re-runs the root agent after a nudge)\ntools:\n  - get_status\nskills:\n  - skills/evidence\noutput_key: evidence\ngenerate_content_config: !include configs/evidence_sampling.yaml\n', 'locator.yaml': 'name: locator\nmodel: gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct\ndescription: "Finds the exact code to change and copies it for the proposers."\ninstruction: !include prompts/locator.md\ninclude_contents: none\ntools:\n  - get_status\nskills:\n  - skills/look\noutput_key: location\ngenerate_content_config: !include configs/sampling.yaml\n', 'prompts/applier.md': 'You are the last engineer of a three-step team that fixes the issue below in the repository at /workspace. A locator found the code to change, and three engineers proposed edits without being able to run anything. Their proposals are at the end. Your job: put the best fix into the SOURCE code, check it once, and submit it. The hidden tests were written from the issue, so its exact names, messages, types and behaviours are the specification.\n\n# RULES THAT ALWAYS HOLD\n- Your tool calls and your time are limited. When either runs out, the working tree is graded as it stands: an edit that exists counts, and an unedited tree scores zero. Edit first.\n- Never create, modify or delete test files (`test_*.py`, `*_test.py`, anything under `tests/`, `pytest.ini`, `conftest.py`).\n- Scratch files go in /tmp only, written with run_command. Any file left under /workspace becomes part of the patch.\n- Never run a whole test suite. Name one test file, and add `-k` when you can.\n\n# HOW TO WORK\n1. Compare the proposals. When two or three of them make the same change (the same lines and the same logic, even if worded or formatted differently), that is the change to apply. When they all differ, apply the one that matches the issue\'s exact names, messages and behaviour best. When none of them fits the issue, write your own fix in the place the locator found.\n2. Apply it with edit_file: the filepath from the proposal, old_string = its SEARCH lines, new_string = its REPLACE lines. If edit_file finds no match, print the region with `sed -n \'120,160p\' path` and copy the exact lines from that output into old_string. A proposal can hold several edits; apply all of them.\n3. Check once: run the closest existing test file, `timeout 150 python -m pytest tests/path/test_x.py -x -q -k keyword` (find it with `find tests -name "*keyword*.py"`), or, when there is none, `python -m py_compile path`. If a test related to your change fails because of it, fix the source once. Failures that were there before your change, or that come from missing fixtures, are not yours: ignore them.\n4. Run `git status --short && git diff`, remove any scratch file under /workspace, restore any changed test file with `git checkout --`, then call submit_patch and confirm that patch_size is above zero.\n5. Reply with one sentence saying which proposal you applied and what it changes.\n\n# EDIT MECHANICS\n- If a tool result says "mandatory input parameters are not present", your call was cut apart because a text ended with a backtick or a stray quote. Do not send the same call again; end every text right after its last character. If it fails twice, make the change with one Python script in a quoted heredoc that asserts the old text occurs exactly once before replacing it.\n- Never repeat an identical failed edit.\n\n# THE ISSUE (verbatim)\n{problem_description}\n\n# REPOSITORY EVIDENCE (lines found by a search for names in the issue)\n{evidence?}\n\n# THE CODE THE LOCATOR FOUND\n{location?}\n\n# PROPOSAL A\n{prop_a?}\n\n# PROPOSAL B\n{prop_b?}\n\n# PROPOSAL C\n{prop_c?}\n', 'prompts/evidence.md': "Your only job is one search of the repository for the agent who fixes the issue after you. Your reply is copied into that agent's instructions and stays in front of it for the whole task, so it must hold real lines from the repository, not your opinions. You will not read code and you will not work on the fix.\n\nChoose two to four exact names from the issue below that the fix most likely touches: functions, methods, classes, options or parameters, written exactly as they appear in the issue or as they would appear in the code. Prefer the most specific names, such as convert_underscores, over common words, such as Header.\n\nCall run_skill_script with skill_name evidence, file_path scripts/evidence.py and positional_args holding those names, one name per item. That is your only call.\n\nThen reply with the lines of its result that best show where the fix belongs and how this code is meant to behave: where the names are defined, tests that use them, and the lines an earlier commit changed around them. Copy each line exactly as it was printed, with its file:line or commit, at most 10 lines and nothing else. If nothing in the result bears on the issue, reply with the single line NOTHING RELEVANT.\n\n# THE ISSUE (verbatim)\n{problem_description}", 'prompts/locator.md': 'You are the locator of a three-step team that fixes the issue below. Your only job is to find the exact place in the SOURCE code that has to change, and to copy that code for the engineers after you. They cannot see the repository: your reply is all they get. You never change code.\n\n# YOUR TOOL\nrun_skill_script with skill_name look, file_path scripts/look.py, and positional_args holding one of:\n- ["def", "NAME"]: where the function or class NAME is defined, with its whole body.\n- ["find", "TEXT"]: source lines (tests left out) that contain TEXT, with file:line.\n- ["tests", "TEXT"]: test lines that contain TEXT, with file:line; tests show how the code is meant to behave.\n- ["show", "FILE", "START", "END"]: lines START to END of FILE.\nExamples of correct calls:\n- run_skill_script with skill_name look, file_path scripts/look.py, positional_args ["def", "split_cells"]\n- run_skill_script with skill_name look, file_path scripts/look.py, positional_args ["show", "rich/segment.py", "120", "160"]\nYou have a few looks. Every result says how many are left, and when they are used up the tool refuses and you reply.\n\n# HOW TO WORK\n1. Read the issue and the repository evidence below. The evidence already shows where the issue\'s names are defined and what earlier commits changed around them; start from it.\n2. Look at the function that most likely produces the wrong behaviour, or, for a new feature, the code the new behaviour belongs next to (often a similar existing option or parameter). `def` is usually the best first look because it shows the whole function.\n3. Follow the call path only as far as needed to be sure where the change goes: the place where the wrong value is produced, not where it is noticed.\n4. Reply.\n\n# YOUR REPLY (exactly this format; it is copied to the engineers)\nLOCATION\nFILE: <path relative to the repository root>\nLINES: <first>-<last>\nWHY: <one sentence: what in this code causes the problem, or where the new behaviour goes>\nCODE:\n<lines first to last, copied exactly as the tool printed them: same indentation, no line numbers, nothing left out in the middle>\nEND CODE\n\nIf the fix also needs a second place (a sibling function, another file, a registration list), add a second LOCATION block in the same format. Prefer whole functions when they are short; keep each block under about eighty lines. Copy only code you have seen in a tool result; never write code from memory.\n\n# THE ISSUE (verbatim)\n{problem_description}\n\n# REPOSITORY EVIDENCE (lines found by a search for names in the issue)\n{evidence?}\n', 'prompts/proposer.md': "You are one of three engineers who each propose a fix for the issue below, on your own. You cannot run or read anything: everything you have is the issue, the repository evidence, and the code the locator copied from the repository (the LOCATION blocks at the end). Your reply is your proposal. An applier compares the three proposals and puts the best one into the repository.\n\n# WHAT A GOOD PROPOSAL DOES\n- The hidden tests were written from the issue, so the exact names, messages, types and behaviours in it are the specification. Use them literally.\n- Fix the cause, in the code shown, not the symptom. Do not special-case the issue's example.\n- Change only what the issue needs. Keep signatures, defaults and backward compatibility unless the issue says otherwise, and follow the style of the code around it.\n- If the issue asks for something new, add it next to the closest existing pattern in the code shown and follow that pattern.\n- Never touch tests.\n\n# YOUR REPLY (exactly this format)\nFor each edit:\nFILE: <path from a LOCATION block>\n<<<<<<< SEARCH\n<a few consecutive lines copied character for character from that LOCATION's CODE, enough to be unique>\n=======\n<the lines that replace them>\n>>>>>>> REPLACE\n\nThen one last line:\nWHY: <one sentence saying what your change does>\n\nThe SEARCH lines must appear in the CODE exactly as written there, with the same indentation. Keep each SEARCH short: the lines you change plus a line or two around them.\n\nExample of the format (another repository):\nFILE: src/pkg/render.py\n<<<<<<< SEARCH\n        if width <= 0:\n            return [text]\n=======\n        if width is None or width <= 0:\n            return [text.strip()]\n>>>>>>> REPLACE\nWHY: a missing or non-positive width now returns the stripped text instead of the raw one.\n\n# THE ISSUE (verbatim)\n{problem_description}\n\n# REPOSITORY EVIDENCE (lines found by a search for names in the issue)\n{evidence?}\n\n# THE CODE THE LOCATOR FOUND\n{location?}\n", 'prop_a.yaml': 'name: prop_a\nmodel: gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct\ndescription: "Proposes a fix (a) as SEARCH/REPLACE blocks, without tools."\ninstruction: !include prompts/proposer.md\ninclude_contents: none\noutput_key: prop_a\ngenerate_content_config: !include configs/prop_a_sampling.yaml\n', 'prop_b.yaml': 'name: prop_b\nmodel: gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct\ndescription: "Proposes a fix (b) as SEARCH/REPLACE blocks, without tools."\ninstruction: !include prompts/proposer.md\ninclude_contents: none\noutput_key: prop_b\ngenerate_content_config: !include configs/prop_b_sampling.yaml\n', 'prop_c.yaml': 'name: prop_c\nmodel: gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct\ndescription: "Proposes a fix (c) as SEARCH/REPLACE blocks, without tools."\ninstruction: !include prompts/proposer.md\ninclude_contents: none\noutput_key: prop_c\ngenerate_content_config: !include configs/prop_c_sampling.yaml\n', 'proposers.yaml': 'name: proposers\nagent_class: ParallelAgent\ndescription: "Three engineers propose a fix each, without tools."\nsub_agents:\n  - config_path: prop_a.yaml\n  - config_path: prop_b.yaml\n  - config_path: prop_c.yaml\n', 'skills/evidence/SKILL.md': "---\nname: evidence\ndescription: Searches the repository's tests, definitions, git history and changelog for a few exact names from the issue. Call run_skill_script with skill_name evidence, file_path scripts/evidence.py and positional_args holding the names.\n---\n\nCall run_skill_script with skill_name evidence, file_path scripts/evidence.py and positional_args holding two to four exact\nnames from the issue, one name per item. It prints what the repository says about each name and takes a few seconds.\n", 'skills/evidence/scripts/evidence.py': '"""Repository evidence for a few exact names from the issue, in one call (arm evid1, scripts/make_mine_arms.py).\n\nRun once per task by the evidence agent through run_skill_script (skill "evidence"), names as positional_args:\n\n    run_skill_script skill_name=evidence file_path=scripts/evidence.py positional_args=["convert_underscores", "Header"]\n\nWhy: r60\'s scouts found good evidence (the right test files, the earlier commits on the buggy lines) but explored for\n2-4 minutes. This script does the same fixed searches in seconds, read-only, inside the repository:\n  - tests that mention the name (git grep in test files),\n  - where the name is defined (def / class lines),\n  - the most recent commits that added or removed the name (git log -S) and the changed lines around it in the newest one,\n  - changelog lines that mention it.\nEvery git call has its own timeout and the whole script stops adding sections after DEADLINE seconds.\n"""\nimport inspect\nimport os\nimport re\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport time\n\nONCE = True  # evid2 (r64): True -> a second call is refused (r63: told to make one call, the agent made 2-17)\nDONE_FILE = os.path.join(".git", "agent", "evidence_done")  # holds the first result, shown again on a refused call\nREPLY = \'Now reply with at most 10 lines of this result, copied exactly, and stop.\'  # evid3 (r65) swaps this: the stage\'s reply becomes its output\nDEADLINE = 8  # r76: seconds for all sections (evid3: 40; large private repos made it the stage\'s main cost)\nCALL_TIMEOUT = 4  # seconds per git call (evid3: 12)\nMAX_NAMES = 5\nOUT_MAX = 4800  # characters; the harness shows about 5000\nTEST_LINES = 6\nDEF_LINES = 3\nCOMMITS = 5\nHUNK_TRIES = 5  # changed lines come from the newest of the first HUNK_TRIES commits that has some\nHUNK_LINES = 18\nWINDOW = 3  # context lines around each changed line that has the name\nLOG_LINES = 3\nLINE_MAX = 150\nTEST_PATHS = [":(glob)**/test/**/*.py", ":(glob)**/tests/**/*.py", ":(glob)**/testing/**/*.py", ":(glob)**/test_*.py", ":(glob)**/*_test.py"]\nPY = [":(glob)**/*.py"]  # history and changed lines: Python files only (docs translations drown everything else)\nLOG_PATHS = [":(glob)**/CHANGELOG*", ":(glob)**/CHANGES*", ":(glob)**/HISTORY*", ":(glob)**/NEWS*", ":(glob)**/release-notes*",\n             ":(glob)**/changelog*"]\n\n\ndef workspace():\n    """The repository root. The skill wrapper chdirs into a temp dir, so look for the directory it started in."""\n    for frame in inspect.stack():\n        cwd = frame.frame.f_locals.get("_orig_cwd")\n        if isinstance(cwd, str) and os.path.isdir(os.path.join(cwd, ".git")):\n            return cwd\n    for cand in (os.environ.get("PYTHONPATH", "").split(os.pathsep)[0], "/workspace", os.getcwd()):\n        if cand and os.path.isdir(os.path.join(cand, ".git")):\n            return cand\n    sys.exit("repository not found")\n\n\ndef git(ws, args, start):\n    """stdout lines of one git call, [] on error, timeout or when the deadline has passed."""\n    left = DEADLINE - (time.time() - start)\n    if left < 1:\n        return None\n    try:\n        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-c", "core.quotepath=off"] + args, cwd=ws, capture_output=True, text=True,\n                           timeout=min(CALL_TIMEOUT, left), stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, errors="replace")\n    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:\n        return []\n    return r.stdout.splitlines() if r.returncode in (0, 1) else []\n\n\ndef short(line):\n    line = line.rstrip()\n    return line if len(line) <= LINE_MAX else line[:LINE_MAX] + " ..."\n\n\ndef names_from_args():\n    raw = [a for a in sys.argv[1:] if a != "--"]\n    names = []\n    for a in raw:\n        for part in re.split(r"[\\n,]", a):\n            n = part.strip().strip("`\'\\"").strip()\n            if len(n) >= 3 and n not in names:\n                names.append(n)\n    return names[:MAX_NAMES]\n\n\ndef newest_hunks(ws, commit, name, start):\n    """Changed lines that contain NAME in COMMIT (Python source, not tests), with WINDOW lines around each, at most\n    HUNK_LINES. Files the commit created or deleted are skipped: a whole new file says nothing about the change."""\n    lines = git(ws, ["show", "--format=", "-U3", "--no-color", commit, "--"] + PY\n                + [":(exclude,glob)" + p[len(":(glob)"):] for p in TEST_PATHS], start)\n    if not lines:\n        return []\n    out, cur_file, hunk, skip = [], None, [], False\n\n    def flush():\n        if skip or not hunk:\n            return\n        hits = [i for i, h in enumerate(hunk) if h[:1] in "+-" and name in h]\n        keep = sorted({j for i in hits for j in range(max(0, i - WINDOW), min(len(hunk), i + WINDOW + 1))})\n        if keep:\n            out.append(f"  {cur_file}:")\n            prev = None\n            for j in keep:\n                if prev is not None and j != prev + 1:\n                    out.append("    ...")\n                out.append("    " + short(hunk[j]))\n                prev = j\n\n    for ln in lines:\n        if ln.startswith("diff --git"):\n            flush()\n            hunk, skip, cur_file = [], False, None\n        elif ln.startswith(("new file", "deleted file")):\n            skip = True\n        elif ln.startswith("+++ "):\n            cur_file = ln[6:] if ln.startswith("+++ b/") else ln[4:]\n        elif ln.startswith(("index ", "--- ", "similarity", "rename ")):\n            continue\n        elif ln.startswith("@@"):\n            flush()\n            hunk = []\n        else:\n            hunk.append(ln)\n    flush()\n    return out[:HUNK_LINES]\n\n\n_RANGE = []\n\n\ndef recent(ws, start):\n    """r76: git log -S only over the last 500 commits (the whole history is slow on large repositories)."""\n    if not _RANGE:\n        revs = git(ws, ["rev-list", "--max-count=500", "HEAD"], start) or []\n        _RANGE.append([revs[-1] + "..HEAD"] if len(revs) == 500 else [])\n    return _RANGE[0]\n\n\ndef section(ws, name, start):\n    out = [f"== {name}"]\n    tests = git(ws, ["grep", "-n", "-I", "-F", "-e", name, "--"] + TEST_PATHS, start)\n    if tests is None:\n        return out + ["  (skipped: time)"]\n    out.append(f"tests mentioning it: {len(tests)} lines" + ("" if tests else " (none)"))\n    out.extend("  " + short(t) for t in tests[:TEST_LINES])\n    if re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*", name):\n        defs = git(ws, ["grep", "-n", "-I", "-E", "-e", rf"^[[:space:]]*(async def|def|class) {name}([^A-Za-z0-9_]|$)", "--", "*.py"], start) or []\n        if defs:\n            out.append("defined at:")\n            out.extend("  " + short(d) for d in defs[:DEF_LINES])\n    log = git(ws, ["log", "-S", name, "--no-merges", "--format=%h %ad %s", "--date=short", f"-n{COMMITS}"] + recent(ws, start) + ["--"] + PY, start)\n    if log is None:\n        return out + ["  (history skipped: time)"]\n    if log:\n        out.append("commits that added or removed it (newest first):")\n        out.extend("  " + short(c) for c in log)\n        for c in log[:HUNK_TRIES]:\n            hunks = newest_hunks(ws, c.split()[0], name, start)\n            if hunks:\n                out.append(f"what {c.split()[0]} changed around it:")\n                out.extend(hunks)\n                break\n    else:\n        out.append("commits that added or removed it: none found")\n    notes = git(ws, ["grep", "-n", "-I", "-i", "-F", "-e", name, "--"] + LOG_PATHS, start) or []\n    if notes:\n        out.append("changelog:")\n        out.extend("  " + short(n) for n in notes[:LOG_LINES])\n    return out\n\n\ndef main():\n    start = time.time()\n    names = names_from_args()\n    if not names:\n        print("NO NAMES: pass 2 to 5 exact names from the issue as positional_args, for example "\n              "[\\"function_name\\", \\"ClassName\\", \\"option_name\\"].")\n        return\n    ws = workspace()\n    done = os.path.join(ws, DONE_FILE)\n    if ONCE and os.path.exists(done):  # also after the harness re-runs the stage with no history: show the result again\n        print("ALREADY DONE: the search already ran and a second call is refused. Its result was:\\n"\n              + open(done, errors="replace").read() + "\\n" + REPLY)\n        return\n    parts = [f"REPOSITORY EVIDENCE for {\', \'.join(names)} (read-only searches; the issue stays the specification)"]\n    for n in names:\n        parts.extend(section(ws, n, start))\n    text = "\\n".join(parts)\n    if len(text) > OUT_MAX:\n        text = text[:OUT_MAX] + "\\n[cut]"\n    if ONCE:\n        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(done), exist_ok=True)\n        open(done, "w").write(text)\n        text += "\\nThis was the only search: a second call is refused. " + REPLY\n    print(text + f"\\n[done in {time.time() - start:.0f} s]")\n\n\nif __name__ == "__main__":\n    main()\n', 'skills/look/SKILL.md': '---\nname: look\ndescription: "Read-only look-around for the locator: def / find / tests / show, a few calls."\n---\n\nCall run_skill_script with skill_name look, file_path scripts/look.py and positional_args ["def", "NAME"], ["find", "TEXT"],\n["tests", "TEXT"] or ["show", "FILE", "START", "END"].\n', 'skills/look/scripts/look.py': '"""Read-only look-around for evpipe\'s locator stage (r92, user 2026-11-12 "build all": the fixed pipeline).\n\n    run_skill_script skill look, file look.py, positional_args:\n      ["find", "TEXT"]                 source lines (tests left out) that contain TEXT, with file:line\n      ["tests", "TEXT"]                test lines that contain TEXT, with file:line\n      ["def", "NAME"]                  where def / class NAME is defined, and its whole body (raw lines)\n      ["show", "FILE", "START", "END"] raw lines START-END of FILE\n\nBounded by structure: after LIMIT calls every call is refused with a line that says to reply now (this model follows\ntool-output refusals, not length rules: memory event-triggered-rules-only; r88\'s drafter with free tools ran 9-78\ncalls). Raw lines are printed without line numbers so the locator can copy them exactly into its LOCATION reply, which\nthe tool-less proposers read. Never writes inside the repository except the call counter in .git/agent."""\nimport ast\nimport inspect\nimport os\nimport re\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\n\nLIMIT = 6\nMAX_LINES = 90\nFIND_LINES, TEST_LINES = 25, 15\nLINE_MAX = 160\nTEST_RE = re.compile(r"(^|/)(tests?|testing)/|(^|/)test_[^/]*\\.py$|_test\\.py$|(^|/)conftest\\.py$")\n\n\ndef workspace():\n    """The repository root. The skill wrapper chdirs into a temp dir, so look for the directory it started in."""\n    for frame in inspect.stack():\n        cwd = frame.frame.f_locals.get("_orig_cwd")\n        if isinstance(cwd, str) and os.path.isdir(os.path.join(cwd, ".git")):\n            return cwd\n    for cand in (os.environ.get("PYTHONPATH", "").split(os.pathsep)[0], "/workspace", os.getcwd()):\n        if cand and os.path.isdir(os.path.join(cand, ".git")):\n            return cand\n    sys.exit("repository not found")\n\n\ndef git(ws, args):\n    try:\n        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-c", "core.quotepath=off"] + args, cwd=ws, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10,\n                           stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, errors="replace")\n    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:\n        return []\n    return r.stdout.splitlines() if r.returncode in (0, 1) else []\n\n\ndef cut(line):\n    line = line.rstrip()\n    return line if len(line) <= LINE_MAX else line[:LINE_MAX] + " ..."\n\n\ndef grep(ws, text, tests):\n    hits = [h for h in git(ws, ["grep", "-n", "-I", "-F", "-e", text, "--", "*.py"]) if bool(TEST_RE.search(h.split(":", 1)[0])) == tests]\n    n = TEST_LINES if tests else FIND_LINES\n    out = [cut(h) for h in hits[:n]]\n    if len(hits) > n:\n        out.append(f"... {len(hits) - n} more lines")\n    return out or [f"no {\'test\' if tests else \'source\'} line contains {text!r}"]\n\n\ndef region(ws, path, a, b):\n    full = os.path.join(ws, path)\n    try:\n        lines = open(full, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").read().splitlines()\n    except OSError as e:\n        return [f"cannot read {path}: {e.strerror}"]\n    a, b = max(1, a), min(len(lines), b)\n    if b < a:\n        return [f"{path} has {len(lines)} lines"]\n    if b - a + 1 > MAX_LINES:\n        b = a + MAX_LINES - 1\n    return [f"== {path} lines {a}-{b} (of {len(lines)}), raw:"] + [l.rstrip("\\n") for l in lines[a - 1:b]]\n\n\ndef define(ws, name):\n    name = name.split(".")[-1].strip("()` ")\n    hits = git(ws, ["grep", "-n", "-I", "-E", "-e", rf"^[[:space:]]*(async def|def|class) {re.escape(name)}([^[:alnum:]_]|$)", "--", "*.py"])  # POSIX ERE: no \\s / \\b\n    hits = [h for h in hits if not TEST_RE.search(h.split(":", 1)[0])]\n    if not hits:\n        return [f"no def or class named {name!r} in the source"]\n    out = []\n    for h in hits[:2]:\n        path, ln = h.split(":", 2)[:2]\n        ln = int(ln)\n        end = ln\n        try:\n            tree = ast.parse(open(os.path.join(ws, path), encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").read())\n            for node in ast.walk(tree):\n                if isinstance(node, (ast.FunctionDef, ast.AsyncFunctionDef, ast.ClassDef)) and node.name == name and node.lineno <= ln <= (node.end_lineno or ln):\n                    start = min([d.lineno for d in node.decorator_list] + [node.lineno])\n                    if start <= ln:\n                        end = node.end_lineno or ln\n                        ln = start\n                        break\n        except (OSError, SyntaxError, ValueError):\n            end = ln + 30\n        out += region(ws, path, ln, end)\n    if len(hits) > 2:\n        out.append("also defined at: " + ", ".join(h.split(":", 2)[0] + ":" + h.split(":", 2)[1] for h in hits[2:6]))\n    return out\n\n\ndef main(argv):\n    ws = workspace()\n    agent = os.path.join(ws, ".git", "agent")\n    os.makedirs(agent, exist_ok=True)\n    counter = os.path.join(agent, "look_count")\n    try:\n        used = int(open(counter).read().strip() or 0)\n    except (OSError, ValueError):\n        used = 0\n    if used >= LIMIT:\n        return ("LOOK LIMIT REACHED: no more looking. Reply now with your LOCATION block, built from the lines you have "\n                "already seen.")\n    args = [a for a in argv if a != "--"]\n    if len(args) == 1 and " " in args[0]:\n        args = args[0].split(None, 3) if args[0].split(None, 1)[0] == "show" else args[0].split(None, 1)\n    verb = (args[0] if args else "").lower()\n    with open(counter, "w") as f:\n        f.write(str(used + 1))\n    left = LIMIT - used - 1\n    if verb in ("find", "tests") and len(args) >= 2:\n        out = grep(ws, " ".join(args[1:]).strip("`\'\\""), verb == "tests")\n    elif verb == "def" and len(args) >= 2:\n        out = define(ws, args[1])\n    elif verb == "show" and len(args) >= 4:\n        try:\n            out = region(ws, args[1].lstrip("./") if not args[1].startswith("/") else os.path.relpath(args[1], ws), int(args[2]), int(args[3]))\n        except ValueError:\n            out = ["show needs FILE START END, for example: show fastapi/routing.py 120 160"]\n    else:\n        out = [\'usage: positional_args ["find", "TEXT"] | ["tests", "TEXT"] | ["def", "NAME"] | ["show", "FILE", "START", "END"]\']\n    out.append(f"[{left} look(s) left]" if left else "[that was the last look: reply now with your LOCATION block]")\n    return "\\n".join(out)\n\n\nif __name__ == "__main__":\n    print(main(sys.argv[1:]))\n'}, 'evloop': {'agent.yaml': '# evloop (r92): evidence -> LoopAgent of 2 rounds [attempt (fresh context, act skill refused after 8 per round), check (diff + related tests)] -> finisher (scripts/make_r92_arms.py)\nname: swe_agent\nagent_class: SequentialAgent\nsub_agents:\n  - config_path: evidence.yaml\n  - config_path: rounds.yaml\n  - config_path: finisher.yaml\n', 'attempt.yaml': 'name: attempt\nmodel: gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct\ndescription: "Makes or improves the fix in one round, from a fresh context and the last report."\ninstruction: !include prompts/attempt.md\ninclude_contents: none\ntools:\n  - edit_file\n  - get_status\nskills:\n  - skills/act\ngenerate_content_config: !include configs/sampling.yaml\n', 'check.yaml': 'name: check\nmodel: gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct\ndescription: "Reports the round\'s diff and the related tests."\ninstruction: !include prompts/check.md\ninclude_contents: none\ntools:\n  - get_status\nskills:\n  - skills/check\noutput_key: report\ngenerate_content_config: !include configs/evidence_sampling.yaml\n', 'configs/evidence_sampling.yaml': '# r76 cheap evidence stage: thinking off; a mechanical job (pick names, call the script once, copy lines).\n# In the installed harness (adk_submission 0.2.13, resolvers/generation.py) any thinking_budget > 0 turns thinking ON and a\n# missing budget is filled with 4096, so include_thoughts: false alone does not switch it off; thinking_budget: 0 does\n# (schema ge=0, limits 0-32768; the request then carries enable_thinking false and no thinking_token_budget).\ntemperature: 0.2\ntop_p: 0.95\nmax_output_tokens: 1024\nthinking_config:\n  thinking_budget: 0\n  include_thoughts: false\n', 'configs/sampling.yaml': 'temperature: 0.2\ntop_p: 0.95\nmax_output_tokens: 8192\nthinking_config:\n  thinking_budget: 4096\n  include_thoughts: true\n', 'eval_config.yaml': 'evaluation:\n  timeout_seconds: 240\n  max_tool_calls: 32\n  max_time_minutes: 8\n  max_turns: 80\n', 'evidence.yaml': 'name: evidence\nmodel: gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct\ndescription: Runs one repository search for names in the issue and copies the lines that matter.\ninstruction: !include prompts/evidence.md\ninclude_contents: none  # fresh: the issue is in its system prompt (the harness re-runs the root agent after a nudge)\ntools:\n  - get_status\nskills:\n  - skills/evidence\noutput_key: evidence\ngenerate_content_config: !include configs/evidence_sampling.yaml\n', 'finisher.yaml': 'name: finisher\nmodel: gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct\ndescription: "Submits the patch."\ninstruction: !include prompts/finisher.md\ninclude_contents: none\ntools:\n  - submit_patch\n  - get_status\ngenerate_content_config: !include configs/evidence_sampling.yaml\n', 'prompts/attempt.md': 'You are an autonomous software engineer who fixes the issue below in rounds. Every round starts fresh: you see only the issue, the repository evidence, and the report of the previous round, which shows what the repository now contains and what the related existing tests say. Your job in this round is to make, or improve, the change to the SOURCE code so that the hidden tests for the issue pass. Those tests were written from the issue text, so its exact names, messages, types and behaviours are the specification.\n\n# YOUR TOOLS\n- run_skill_script with skill_name act, file_path scripts/act.py, and positional_args holding one shell command, for example ["git grep -n -F convert_underscores -- \'*.py\' | head -20"] or ["sed -n \'120,160p\' fastapi/routing.py"]. It runs in the repository root. It counts this round\'s commands, tells you how many are left, and refuses when they are used up.\n- edit_file with a filepath relative to the repository root, an old_string copied character for character from lines you printed (same indentation, no line numbers), and a new_string.\n- get_status.\n\n# THIS ROUND\n- If the report below is empty or says NO SOURCE EDIT YET: find the place (start from the evidence and the issue\'s exact names, read the function with `sed -n`), and make your best edit early. A wrong edit can be corrected next round; a round without an edit is wasted.\n- If the report shows a diff and related tests failing because of it: read the failure and fix the source.\n- If the report shows a diff and the related tests passing: compare the diff with the issue. Complete what is missing (exact names and messages, every edge case the issue mentions, parallel implementations such as sync and async variants, re-exports in `__init__.py` or `__all__`). If nothing is missing, change nothing.\n- End the round with one line: ROUND DONE: <what you changed, or "nothing to change">. Do not call submit_patch: the team submits after the last round.\n\n# RULES THAT ALWAYS HOLD\n- When time or calls run out, the working tree is graded as it stands: an edit that exists counts, and an unedited tree scores zero.\n- Never create, modify or delete test files (`test_*.py`, `*_test.py`, anything under `tests/`, `pytest.ini`, `conftest.py`).\n- Scratch files go in /tmp only. Any file left in the repository becomes part of the patch.\n- Never run a whole test suite. Name one test file, and add `-k` when you can: `timeout 150 python -m pytest tests/path/test_x.py -x -q -k keyword`.\n- Fix the cause, not the symptom, and do not special-case the issue\'s example. Keep signatures, defaults and backward compatibility unless the issue says otherwise, and follow the surrounding style.\n- For documentation-code tasks (for example FastAPI), the executable code under `docs_src/` is what you edit.\n- If a tool result says "mandatory input parameters are not present", your call was cut apart because a text ended with a backtick or a stray quote. Do not send the same call again; end every text right after its last character.\n- If edit_file finds no match, print the exact region and copy the lines from that output. Never repeat an identical failed edit.\n\n# THE ISSUE (verbatim)\n{problem_description}\n\n# REPOSITORY EVIDENCE (lines found by a search for names in the issue)\n{evidence?}\n\n# REPORT OF THE PREVIOUS ROUND (empty in the first round)\n{report?}\n', 'prompts/check.md': 'Your only job is to report what the last round left in the repository. You do not read or change code.\n\nCall run_skill_script with skill_name check, file_path scripts/check.py and no other arguments. That is your only call.\n\nThen reply with its whole output, copied exactly, and nothing else.\n', 'prompts/evidence.md': "Your only job is one search of the repository for the agent who fixes the issue after you. Your reply is copied into that agent's instructions and stays in front of it for the whole task, so it must hold real lines from the repository, not your opinions. You will not read code and you will not work on the fix.\n\nChoose two to four exact names from the issue below that the fix most likely touches: functions, methods, classes, options or parameters, written exactly as they appear in the issue or as they would appear in the code. Prefer the most specific names, such as convert_underscores, over common words, such as Header.\n\nCall run_skill_script with skill_name evidence, file_path scripts/evidence.py and positional_args holding those names, one name per item. That is your only call.\n\nThen reply with the lines of its result that best show where the fix belongs and how this code is meant to behave: where the names are defined, tests that use them, and the lines an earlier commit changed around them. Copy each line exactly as it was printed, with its file:line or commit, at most 10 lines and nothing else. If nothing in the result bears on the issue, reply with the single line NOTHING RELEVANT.\n\n# THE ISSUE (verbatim)\n{problem_description}", 'prompts/finisher.md': 'The work on the issue is finished and the change is in the repository. Your only job is to submit it.\n\nCall submit_patch once. Then reply with one sentence and make no other call.\n', 'rounds.yaml': 'name: rounds\nagent_class: LoopAgent\ndescription: "Two rounds: an attempt in a fresh context, then a report of the diff and the related tests."\nmax_iterations: 2\nsub_agents:\n  - config_path: attempt.yaml\n  - config_path: check.yaml\n', 'skills/act/SKILL.md': '---\nname: act\ndescription: "One shell command in the repository root per call; a few per round."\n---\n\nCall run_skill_script with skill_name act, file_path scripts/act.py and positional_args holding one shell command.\n', 'skills/act/scripts/act.py': '"""Shell for evloop\'s attempt rounds (r92, user 2026-11-12 "build all": LoopAgent rounds with fresh contexts).\n\n    run_skill_script skill act, file act.py, positional_args ["<one shell command>"]\n\nRuns the command with bash in the repository root (timeout 120 s, output cut to 4,000 characters) and counts the\ncalls of the current round (.git/agent/round, advanced by check.py). After LIMIT calls in a round every call is refused\nwith a line that says to make the edit now and end the round: a refusal is the one limit this model follows (memory\nevent-triggered-rules-only), and the shared call budget has no per-agent limit (r88: a free drafter ran 9-78 calls).\n\'/workspace\' and \'/tmp\' in the command are mapped as the harness maps them in run_command (swegemma\'s subprocess\nsandbox rewrites them only in command strings, and this command reaches bash through Python)."""\nimport inspect\nimport os\nimport re\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport tempfile\n\nLIMIT = 8\nTIMEOUT = 120\nOUT_MAX = 4000\n\n\ndef workspace():\n    for frame in inspect.stack():\n        cwd = frame.frame.f_locals.get("_orig_cwd")\n        if isinstance(cwd, str) and os.path.isdir(os.path.join(cwd, ".git")):\n            return cwd\n    for cand in (os.environ.get("PYTHONPATH", "").split(os.pathsep)[0], "/workspace", os.getcwd()):\n        if cand and os.path.isdir(os.path.join(cand, ".git")):\n            return cand\n    sys.exit("repository not found")\n\n\ndef read_int(path, default):\n    try:\n        return int(open(path).read().strip() or default)\n    except (OSError, ValueError):\n        return default\n\n\ndef main(argv):\n    ws = workspace()\n    agent = os.path.join(ws, ".git", "agent")\n    os.makedirs(agent, exist_ok=True)\n    rnd = read_int(os.path.join(agent, "round"), 1)\n    counter = os.path.join(agent, f"act_count_{rnd}")\n    used = read_int(counter, 0)\n    if used >= LIMIT:\n        return ("ROUND LIMIT REACHED: this round\'s commands are used up. If you have not edited the source yet, make "\n                "your best edit now with edit_file. Then reply with one line, ROUND DONE: <what you changed>.")\n    cmd = " ".join(a for a in argv if a != "--").strip()\n    if not cmd:\n        return \'usage: positional_args ["<one shell command>"], for example ["git grep -n -F convert_underscores -- \\\'*.py\\\' | head -20"]\'\n    with open(counter, "w") as f:\n        f.write(str(used + 1))\n    tmp = tempfile.gettempdir()\n    cmd = re.sub(r"(?<![\\w.-])/workspace(?![\\w.-])", ws, cmd)\n    cmd = re.sub(r"(?<![\\w.-])/tmp(?![\\w.-])", tmp, cmd)\n    try:\n        r = subprocess.run(["/bin/bash", "-c", cmd], cwd=ws, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=TIMEOUT,\n                           stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, errors="replace")\n        out = (r.stdout or "") + (("\\n[stderr]\\n" + r.stderr) if r.stderr.strip() else "") + f"\\n[exit {r.returncode}]"\n    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:\n        out = f"[timed out after {TIMEOUT} s]"\n    if len(out) > OUT_MAX:\n        out = out[:OUT_MAX // 2] + "\\n[... cut ...]\\n" + out[-OUT_MAX // 2:]\n    left = LIMIT - used - 1\n    return out + (f"\\n[{left} command(s) left in this round]" if left else\n                  "\\n[that was this round\'s last command: edit now if you have not, then reply ROUND DONE]")\n\n\nif __name__ == "__main__":\n    print(main(sys.argv[1:]))\n', 'skills/check/SKILL.md': '---\nname: check\ndescription: "The round report: the source diff and the related existing tests."\n---\n\nCall run_skill_script with skill_name check and file_path scripts/check.py, with no other arguments.\n', 'skills/check/scripts/check.py': '"""Round report for evloop\'s check stage (r92, user 2026-11-12 "build all": LoopAgent rounds with fresh contexts).\n\n    run_skill_script skill act, file check.py (no arguments)\n\nPrints what the round left in the repository and what the related existing tests say, then advances the round\n(.git/agent/round) so act.py gives the next round fresh commands. The next attempt starts with an empty context and\nsees only this report (copied by the check stage into its output_key), the issue and the evidence:\n  - the diff of the source against the baseline (tests and .git left out), cut to DIFF_LINES;\n  - up to TESTS_MAX existing test files that import a changed module or are named after it (scripts/agent_team/vote.py\'s\n    rule), run with pytest -x -q, and the summary line plus the start of the first failure.\nCalled a second time in the same round, it prints the saved report again. A marker file .git/agent/NO_TESTS (made\nonly by the local simulator, which must not run task code) skips the tests."""\nimport inspect\nimport os\nimport re\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\n\nDIFF_LINES = 45\nTESTS_MAX = 2\nTEST_TIMEOUT = 60\nFAIL_LINES = 14\nTEST_RE = re.compile(r"(^|/)(tests?|testing)/|(^|/)test_[^/]*\\.py$|_test\\.py$|(^|/)conftest\\.py$|(^|/)pytest\\.ini$")\n\n\ndef workspace():\n    for frame in inspect.stack():\n        cwd = frame.frame.f_locals.get("_orig_cwd")\n        if isinstance(cwd, str) and os.path.isdir(os.path.join(cwd, ".git")):\n            return cwd\n    for cand in (os.environ.get("PYTHONPATH", "").split(os.pathsep)[0], "/workspace", os.getcwd()):\n        if cand and os.path.isdir(os.path.join(cand, ".git")):\n            return cand\n    sys.exit("repository not found")\n\n\ndef sh(args, cwd, timeout=60, env=None):\n    try:\n        r = subprocess.run(args, cwd=cwd, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,\n                           errors="replace", env=env)\n        return r.returncode, r.stdout, r.stderr\n    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:\n        return -9, "", "timed out"\n\n\ndef source_diff(ws):\n    """git diff of source files against the baseline, through a throw-away index (new files counted, real index kept)."""\n    import tempfile\n    ref = "_swegemma_baseline" if sh(["git", "rev-parse", "-q", "--verify", "_swegemma_baseline"], ws)[0] == 0 else "HEAD"\n    fd, idx = tempfile.mkstemp(prefix="check_idx_")\n    os.close(fd)\n    try:\n        real = os.path.join(ws, ".git", "index")\n        if os.path.exists(real):\n            with open(real, "rb") as s, open(idx, "wb") as d:\n                d.write(s.read())\n        else:\n            os.remove(idx)\n        env = {**os.environ, "GIT_INDEX_FILE": idx}\n        sh(["git", "add", "-A", "-N", "."], ws, env=env)\n        _, out, _ = sh(["git", "diff", "--no-color", ref], ws, env=env)\n    finally:\n        if os.path.exists(idx):\n            os.remove(idx)\n    keep = []\n    for block in re.split(r"(?m)^(?=diff --git )", out):\n        m = re.match(r"diff --git a/(\\S+) b/(\\S+)", block)\n        if m and not TEST_RE.search(m.group(2)) and not os.path.basename(m.group(2)).startswith(".adk_exec_"):\n            keep.append(block)\n    return "".join(keep)\n\n\ndef select_tests(ws, sources):\n    scored = []\n    for top in ("tests", "test", "testing"):\n        for dirpath, _, files in os.walk(os.path.join(ws, top)):\n            for fn in files:\n                if not (fn.startswith("test_") and fn.endswith(".py")):\n                    continue\n                path = os.path.join(dirpath, fn)\n                try:\n                    text = open(path, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").read()\n                except OSError:\n                    continue\n                s = 0\n                for src in sources:\n                    mod = src[:-3].replace("/", ".").removesuffix(".__init__")\n                    if mod.startswith("src."):\n                        mod = mod[4:]\n                    pkg, _, stem = mod.rpartition(".")\n                    if re.search(rf"\\b(import|from)\\s+{re.escape(mod)}\\b", text) or (\n                            pkg and re.search(rf"from\\s+{re.escape(pkg)}\\s+import\\s+[^\\n]*\\b{re.escape(stem)}\\b", text)):\n                        s += 2\n                    if stem and stem not in ("__init__", "utils", "main") and stem in fn:\n                        s += 3\n                if s:\n                    scored.append((-s, len(text), os.path.relpath(path, ws)))\n    return [p for _, _, p in sorted(scored)[:TESTS_MAX]]\n\n\ndef report(ws, rnd):\n    diff = source_diff(ws)\n    out = [f"ROUND {rnd} REPORT"]\n    if not diff.strip():\n        return out + ["NO SOURCE EDIT YET: the source is unchanged. The next round must edit the most likely place early."]\n    lines = diff.splitlines()\n    files = re.findall(r"^\\+\\+\\+ b/(\\S+)", diff, re.M)\n    out.append("changed: " + ", ".join(files))\n    out.append("diff:")\n    out += lines[:DIFF_LINES] + ([f"... {len(lines) - DIFF_LINES} more diff lines"] if len(lines) > DIFF_LINES else [])\n    if os.path.exists(os.path.join(ws, ".git", "agent", "NO_TESTS")):\n        return out + ["related tests: skipped (simulator)"]\n    tests = select_tests(ws, [f for f in files if f.endswith(".py")])\n    if not tests:\n        return out + ["related tests: none found for the changed modules"]\n    code, o, e = sh([sys.executable, "-m", "pytest", *tests, "-x", "-q", "-p", "no:cacheprovider", "-o", "timeout=0"], ws, TEST_TIMEOUT,\n                    env={**os.environ, "PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE": "1"})\n    text = (o or "") + (e or "")\n    summary = next((l for l in reversed(text.splitlines()) if re.search(r"\\d+ (passed|failed|errors?)", l)), "")\n    out.append(f"related tests ({\', \'.join(tests)}): " + (summary.strip("= ") if summary else ("timed out" if code == -9 else f"no summary (exit {code})")))\n    if code not in (0, -9) and ("failed" in summary or "error" in summary):\n        first = re.split(r"(?m)^_{5,} ", text, maxsplit=1)\n        body = (first[1] if len(first) > 1 else text).splitlines()\n        out.append("first failure:")\n        out += ["  " + l[:160] for l in body[:FAIL_LINES]]\n    return out\n\n\ndef main():\n    ws = workspace()\n    agent = os.path.join(ws, ".git", "agent")\n    os.makedirs(agent, exist_ok=True)\n    rpath = os.path.join(agent, "round")\n    try:\n        rnd = int(open(rpath).read().strip() or 1)\n    except (OSError, ValueError):\n        rnd = 1\n    import hashlib\n    digest = hashlib.sha1(source_diff(ws).encode("utf-8", "replace")).hexdigest()\n    prev = os.path.join(agent, f"report_{rnd - 1}")\n    # a second call with nothing done since the last report (no command this round, same diff): the same report again\n    if rnd > 1 and not os.path.exists(os.path.join(agent, f"act_count_{rnd}")) and os.path.exists(prev + ".diff") \\\n            and open(prev + ".diff").read() == digest:\n        return open(prev, errors="replace").read()\n    text = "\\n".join(report(ws, rnd))\n    with open(os.path.join(agent, f"report_{rnd}"), "w") as f:\n        f.write(text)\n    with open(os.path.join(agent, f"report_{rnd}.diff"), "w") as f:\n        f.write(digest)\n    with open(rpath, "w") as f:\n        f.write(str(rnd + 1))\n    return text\n\n\nif __name__ == "__main__":\n    try:\n        print(main())\n    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001  the check stage must always get a report\n        print(f"ROUND REPORT ERROR {type(e).__name__}: {e}")\n', 'skills/evidence/SKILL.md': "---\nname: evidence\ndescription: Searches the repository's tests, definitions, git history and changelog for a few exact names from the issue. Call run_skill_script with skill_name evidence, file_path scripts/evidence.py and positional_args holding the names.\n---\n\nCall run_skill_script with skill_name evidence, file_path scripts/evidence.py and positional_args holding two to four exact\nnames from the issue, one name per item. It prints what the repository says about each name and takes a few seconds.\n", 'skills/evidence/scripts/evidence.py': '"""Repository evidence for a few exact names from the issue, in one call (arm evid1, scripts/make_mine_arms.py).\n\nRun once per task by the evidence agent through run_skill_script (skill "evidence"), names as positional_args:\n\n    run_skill_script skill_name=evidence file_path=scripts/evidence.py positional_args=["convert_underscores", "Header"]\n\nWhy: r60\'s scouts found good evidence (the right test files, the earlier commits on the buggy lines) but explored for\n2-4 minutes. This script does the same fixed searches in seconds, read-only, inside the repository:\n  - tests that mention the name (git grep in test files),\n  - where the name is defined (def / class lines),\n  - the most recent commits that added or removed the name (git log -S) and the changed lines around it in the newest one,\n  - changelog lines that mention it.\nEvery git call has its own timeout and the whole script stops adding sections after DEADLINE seconds.\n"""\nimport inspect\nimport os\nimport re\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport time\n\nONCE = True  # evid2 (r64): True -> a second call is refused (r63: told to make one call, the agent made 2-17)\nDONE_FILE = os.path.join(".git", "agent", "evidence_done")  # holds the first result, shown again on a refused call\nREPLY = \'Now reply with at most 10 lines of this result, copied exactly, and stop.\'  # evid3 (r65) swaps this: the stage\'s reply becomes its output\nDEADLINE = 8  # r76: seconds for all sections (evid3: 40; large private repos made it the stage\'s main cost)\nCALL_TIMEOUT = 4  # seconds per git call (evid3: 12)\nMAX_NAMES = 5\nOUT_MAX = 4800  # characters; the harness shows about 5000\nTEST_LINES = 6\nDEF_LINES = 3\nCOMMITS = 5\nHUNK_TRIES = 5  # changed lines come from the newest of the first HUNK_TRIES commits that has some\nHUNK_LINES = 18\nWINDOW = 3  # context lines around each changed line that has the name\nLOG_LINES = 3\nLINE_MAX = 150\nTEST_PATHS = [":(glob)**/test/**/*.py", ":(glob)**/tests/**/*.py", ":(glob)**/testing/**/*.py", ":(glob)**/test_*.py", ":(glob)**/*_test.py"]\nPY = [":(glob)**/*.py"]  # history and changed lines: Python files only (docs translations drown everything else)\nLOG_PATHS = [":(glob)**/CHANGELOG*", ":(glob)**/CHANGES*", ":(glob)**/HISTORY*", ":(glob)**/NEWS*", ":(glob)**/release-notes*",\n             ":(glob)**/changelog*"]\n\n\ndef workspace():\n    """The repository root. The skill wrapper chdirs into a temp dir, so look for the directory it started in."""\n    for frame in inspect.stack():\n        cwd = frame.frame.f_locals.get("_orig_cwd")\n        if isinstance(cwd, str) and os.path.isdir(os.path.join(cwd, ".git")):\n            return cwd\n    for cand in (os.environ.get("PYTHONPATH", "").split(os.pathsep)[0], "/workspace", os.getcwd()):\n        if cand and os.path.isdir(os.path.join(cand, ".git")):\n            return cand\n    sys.exit("repository not found")\n\n\ndef git(ws, args, start):\n    """stdout lines of one git call, [] on error, timeout or when the deadline has passed."""\n    left = DEADLINE - (time.time() - start)\n    if left < 1:\n        return None\n    try:\n        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-c", "core.quotepath=off"] + args, cwd=ws, capture_output=True, text=True,\n                           timeout=min(CALL_TIMEOUT, left), stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, errors="replace")\n    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:\n        return []\n    return r.stdout.splitlines() if r.returncode in (0, 1) else []\n\n\ndef short(line):\n    line = line.rstrip()\n    return line if len(line) <= LINE_MAX else line[:LINE_MAX] + " ..."\n\n\ndef names_from_args():\n    raw = [a for a in sys.argv[1:] if a != "--"]\n    names = []\n    for a in raw:\n        for part in re.split(r"[\\n,]", a):\n            n = part.strip().strip("`\'\\"").strip()\n            if len(n) >= 3 and n not in names:\n                names.append(n)\n    return names[:MAX_NAMES]\n\n\ndef newest_hunks(ws, commit, name, start):\n    """Changed lines that contain NAME in COMMIT (Python source, not tests), with WINDOW lines around each, at most\n    HUNK_LINES. Files the commit created or deleted are skipped: a whole new file says nothing about the change."""\n    lines = git(ws, ["show", "--format=", "-U3", "--no-color", commit, "--"] + PY\n                + [":(exclude,glob)" + p[len(":(glob)"):] for p in TEST_PATHS], start)\n    if not lines:\n        return []\n    out, cur_file, hunk, skip = [], None, [], False\n\n    def flush():\n        if skip or not hunk:\n            return\n        hits = [i for i, h in enumerate(hunk) if h[:1] in "+-" and name in h]\n        keep = sorted({j for i in hits for j in range(max(0, i - WINDOW), min(len(hunk), i + WINDOW + 1))})\n        if keep:\n            out.append(f"  {cur_file}:")\n            prev = None\n            for j in keep:\n                if prev is not None and j != prev + 1:\n                    out.append("    ...")\n                out.append("    " + short(hunk[j]))\n                prev = j\n\n    for ln in lines:\n        if ln.startswith("diff --git"):\n            flush()\n            hunk, skip, cur_file = [], False, None\n        elif ln.startswith(("new file", "deleted file")):\n            skip = True\n        elif ln.startswith("+++ "):\n            cur_file = ln[6:] if ln.startswith("+++ b/") else ln[4:]\n        elif ln.startswith(("index ", "--- ", "similarity", "rename ")):\n            continue\n        elif ln.startswith("@@"):\n            flush()\n            hunk = []\n        else:\n            hunk.append(ln)\n    flush()\n    return out[:HUNK_LINES]\n\n\n_RANGE = []\n\n\ndef recent(ws, start):\n    """r76: git log -S only over the last 500 commits (the whole history is slow on large repositories)."""\n    if not _RANGE:\n        revs = git(ws, ["rev-list", "--max-count=500", "HEAD"], start) or []\n        _RANGE.append([revs[-1] + "..HEAD"] if len(revs) == 500 else [])\n    return _RANGE[0]\n\n\ndef section(ws, name, start):\n    out = [f"== {name}"]\n    tests = git(ws, ["grep", "-n", "-I", "-F", "-e", name, "--"] + TEST_PATHS, start)\n    if tests is None:\n        return out + ["  (skipped: time)"]\n    out.append(f"tests mentioning it: {len(tests)} lines" + ("" if tests else " (none)"))\n    out.extend("  " + short(t) for t in tests[:TEST_LINES])\n    if re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*", name):\n        defs = git(ws, ["grep", "-n", "-I", "-E", "-e", rf"^[[:space:]]*(async def|def|class) {name}([^A-Za-z0-9_]|$)", "--", "*.py"], start) or []\n        if defs:\n            out.append("defined at:")\n            out.extend("  " + short(d) for d in defs[:DEF_LINES])\n    log = git(ws, ["log", "-S", name, "--no-merges", "--format=%h %ad %s", "--date=short", f"-n{COMMITS}"] + recent(ws, start) + ["--"] + PY, start)\n    if log is None:\n        return out + ["  (history skipped: time)"]\n    if log:\n        out.append("commits that added or removed it (newest first):")\n        out.extend("  " + short(c) for c in log)\n        for c in log[:HUNK_TRIES]:\n            hunks = newest_hunks(ws, c.split()[0], name, start)\n            if hunks:\n                out.append(f"what {c.split()[0]} changed around it:")\n                out.extend(hunks)\n                break\n    else:\n        out.append("commits that added or removed it: none found")\n    notes = git(ws, ["grep", "-n", "-I", "-i", "-F", "-e", name, "--"] + LOG_PATHS, start) or []\n    if notes:\n        out.append("changelog:")\n        out.extend("  " + short(n) for n in notes[:LOG_LINES])\n    return out\n\n\ndef main():\n    start = time.time()\n    names = names_from_args()\n    if not names:\n        print("NO NAMES: pass 2 to 5 exact names from the issue as positional_args, for example "\n              "[\\"function_name\\", \\"ClassName\\", \\"option_name\\"].")\n        return\n    ws = workspace()\n    done = os.path.join(ws, DONE_FILE)\n    if ONCE and os.path.exists(done):  # also after the harness re-runs the stage with no history: show the result again\n        print("ALREADY DONE: the search already ran and a second call is refused. Its result was:\\n"\n              + open(done, errors="replace").read() + "\\n" + REPLY)\n        return\n    parts = [f"REPOSITORY EVIDENCE for {\', \'.join(names)} (read-only searches; the issue stays the specification)"]\n    for n in names:\n        parts.extend(section(ws, n, start))\n    text = "\\n".join(parts)\n    if len(text) > OUT_MAX:\n        text = text[:OUT_MAX] + "\\n[cut]"\n    if ONCE:\n        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(done), exist_ok=True)\n        open(done, "w").write(text)\n        text += "\\nThis was the only search: a second call is refused. " + REPLY\n    print(text + f"\\n[done in {time.time() - start:.0f} s]")\n\n\nif __name__ == "__main__":\n    main()\n'}, 'evroute': {'agent.yaml': '# evroute (r92): evidence -> router (one transfer_to_agent) -> bug_fixer / feature_fixer / docs_fixer (scripts/make_r92_arms.py)\nname: swe_agent\nagent_class: SequentialAgent\nsub_agents:\n  - config_path: evidence.yaml\n  - config_path: router.yaml\n', 'bug_fixer.yaml': 'name: bug_fixer\nmodel: gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct\ndescription: "Fixes bugs: something the code already does is wrong."\ninstruction: !include prompts/bug_fixer.md\ntools:\n  - run_command\n  - read_file\n  - edit_file\n  - write_file\n  - get_status\n  - submit_patch\ndisallow_transfer_to_parent: true\ndisallow_transfer_to_peers: true\ngenerate_content_config: !include configs/sampling.yaml\n', 'configs/evidence_sampling.yaml': '# r76 cheap evidence stage: thinking off; a mechanical job (pick names, call the script once, copy lines).\n# In the installed harness (adk_submission 0.2.13, resolvers/generation.py) any thinking_budget > 0 turns thinking ON and a\n# missing budget is filled with 4096, so include_thoughts: false alone does not switch it off; thinking_budget: 0 does\n# (schema ge=0, limits 0-32768; the request then carries enable_thinking false and no thinking_token_budget).\ntemperature: 0.2\ntop_p: 0.95\nmax_output_tokens: 1024\nthinking_config:\n  thinking_budget: 0\n  include_thoughts: false\n', 'configs/sampling.yaml': 'temperature: 0.2\ntop_p: 0.95\nmax_output_tokens: 8192\nthinking_config:\n  thinking_budget: 4096\n  include_thoughts: true\n', 'docs_fixer.yaml': 'name: docs_fixer\nmodel: gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct\ndescription: "Fixes documentation example code such as docs_src."\ninstruction: !include prompts/docs_fixer.md\ntools:\n  - run_command\n  - read_file\n  - edit_file\n  - write_file\n  - get_status\n  - submit_patch\ndisallow_transfer_to_parent: true\ndisallow_transfer_to_peers: true\ngenerate_content_config: !include configs/sampling.yaml\n', 'eval_config.yaml': 'evaluation:\n  timeout_seconds: 240\n  max_tool_calls: 29\n  max_time_minutes: 8\n  max_turns: 80\n', 'evidence.yaml': 'name: evidence\nmodel: gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct\ndescription: Runs one repository search for names in the issue and copies the lines that matter.\ninstruction: !include prompts/evidence.md\ninclude_contents: none  # fresh: the issue is in its system prompt (the harness re-runs the root agent after a nudge)\ntools:\n  - get_status\nskills:\n  - skills/evidence\noutput_key: evidence\ngenerate_content_config: !include configs/evidence_sampling.yaml\n', 'feature_fixer.yaml': 'name: feature_fixer\nmodel: gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct\ndescription: "Adds features: something the code does not do yet."\ninstruction: !include prompts/feature_fixer.md\ntools:\n  - run_command\n  - read_file\n  - edit_file\n  - write_file\n  - get_status\n  - submit_patch\ndisallow_transfer_to_parent: true\ndisallow_transfer_to_peers: true\ngenerate_content_config: !include configs/sampling.yaml\n', 'prompts/bug_fixer.md': 'You are an autonomous software engineer who fixes bugs. The issue in the task is a bug: something the code already does is wrong. Make the smallest correct change to the SOURCE code in /workspace so that the hidden tests for the issue pass. The hidden tests were written from the issue text, so its exact names, messages, types and behaviours are the specification.\n\n# RULES THAT ALWAYS HOLD\n- Your tool calls and your time are limited. When either runs out, the working tree is graded as it stands: an edit that exists counts, even without `submit_patch`, and an unedited tree scores zero.\n- Never create, modify or delete test files (`test_*.py`, `*_test.py`, anything under `tests/`, `pytest.ini`, `conftest.py`). That is an automatic failure.\n- Scratch files go in /tmp only, written with run_command (`cat > /tmp/check.py <<\'EOF\'`). Any file left under /workspace becomes part of the graded patch.\n- Never run a whole test suite. Name one test file, and add `-k` when you can.\n- Never look outside /workspace. Every dependency is already installed.\n\n# HOW A BUG GETS FIXED HERE\n1. Pin down the wrong behaviour (no tool call): the input that triggers it, what happens now, what should happen instead, and every exact name, message and type the issue gives.\n2. Find where the wrong behaviour is produced. Start from the repository evidence at the end and the issue\'s exact names; search with `git grep -n -F` for literal names, a few searches in one run_command, each kept to about twenty lines. Read the function with `sed -n` and its line range, plus one caller or test that shows the intended contract.\n3. Reproduce it once, early: a short script in /tmp that runs the triggering input and prints the result, run with `python3 /tmp/check.py`. If the issue has a code snippet, that snippet is the script. Use the output to confirm which function returns the wrong value.\n4. Edit as soon as you know the place. The right place is where the wrong value is produced, not where it is noticed. Fix the cause and do not special-case the issue\'s example. Keep signatures, defaults and backward compatibility, and follow the surrounding style and error idioms. If the same logic exists in parallel implementations (sync and async variants, several backends, typing overloads), fix every one.\n5. Verify: run your /tmp script again, then the closest existing test file, `timeout 150 python -m pytest tests/path/test_x.py -x -q -k keyword` (find it with `find tests -name "*keyword*.py"`). If a related test fails because of your change, fix the source once and rerun once. Failures from missing fixtures or unrelated imports were there before; ignore them and never repair tests or the environment.\n6. Finish: `git status --short && git diff` in one call; remove scratch files under /workspace and restore any changed test file with `git checkout --`; call `submit_patch` and confirm that patch_size is above zero; end with one sentence.\n\n# EDIT MECHANICS\n- edit_file: old_string copied character for character from lines you printed (same indentation, no line numbers), a short unique match.\n- If a tool result says "mandatory input parameters are not present", your call was cut apart because a text ended with a backtick or a stray quote. Do not send the same call again; end every text right after its last character. If it fails twice, make the change with one Python script in a quoted heredoc that asserts the old text occurs exactly once before replacing it.\n- If edit_file finds no match or several, print the exact region and retry with a smaller unique snippet. Never repeat an identical failed edit.\n\n# WHEN IN DOUBT\n- Several possible places: choose the one where the wrong value is produced.\n- A fix did not work: make a different, smaller change.\n- Never conclude that nothing needs to change. Every task needs a non-empty source patch.\n\n# REPOSITORY EVIDENCE (found before you started; it stays here for the whole task)\nLines copied from a search of this repository for names in the issue: where they are defined, tests that use them, and lines that earlier commits changed around them. The issue stays the specification.\n{evidence?}\n', 'prompts/docs_fixer.md': 'You are an autonomous software engineer who fixes documentation code. The issue in the task is about the example programs that the documentation shows, such as FastAPI\'s `docs_src/` files. Those programs are real code: the hidden tests import and run them (for FastAPI, the files under `tests/test_tutorial/` import the matching `docs_src/` modules). Make the smallest correct change so that the hidden tests for the issue pass. The issue\'s exact names, messages and behaviours are the specification.\n\n# RULES THAT ALWAYS HOLD\n- Your tool calls and your time are limited. When either runs out, the working tree is graded as it stands: an edit that exists counts, even without `submit_patch`, and an unedited tree scores zero.\n- Never create, modify or delete test files (`test_*.py`, `*_test.py`, anything under `tests/`, `pytest.ini`, `conftest.py`). That is an automatic failure.\n- Scratch files go in /tmp only, written with run_command. Any file left under /workspace becomes part of the graded patch.\n- Never run a whole test suite. Name one test file, and add `-k` when you can.\n- Never look outside /workspace. Every dependency is already installed.\n\n# HOW A DOCUMENTATION-CODE CHANGE IS MADE HERE\n1. Pin down the request (no tool call): which tutorial or example, what must change in it, and every exact name, message and output the issue gives.\n2. Find the example files. Start from the repository evidence at the end and the issue\'s names: `git grep -n -F` in `docs_src/` and in `docs/en/` (the markdown pages include the example files by path), and `ls` the matching `docs_src/<topic>/` folder. One example often exists in several variants side by side (for example `tutorial001.py`, `tutorial001_py39.py`, `tutorial001_py310.py`, `tutorial001_an.py`, `tutorial001_an_py39.py`): list them all.\n3. Find the test that imports them: `git grep -n -F "docs_src.<topic>" tests/` and read the closest test file to see what it expects.\n4. Make the change in every variant of the example, keeping each variant\'s own style (its type-hint syntax, `Annotated` or not). If the example relies on library code that must change too, change that source as well.\n5. Verify: run the matching test file, `timeout 150 python -m pytest tests/test_tutorial/<topic>/test_x.py -x -q`. If it fails because of your change, fix the example once and rerun once. Failures from missing fixtures or unrelated imports were there before; ignore them.\n6. Finish: `git status --short && git diff` in one call; remove scratch files under /workspace and restore any changed test file with `git checkout --`; call `submit_patch` and confirm that patch_size is above zero; end with one sentence.\n\n# EDIT MECHANICS\n- edit_file: old_string copied character for character from lines you printed (same indentation, no line numbers), a short unique match. write_file only for a genuinely new example file the issue requires.\n- If a tool result says "mandatory input parameters are not present", your call was cut apart because a text ended with a backtick or a stray quote. Do not send the same call again; end every text right after its last character. If it fails twice, make the change with one Python script in a quoted heredoc that asserts the old text occurs exactly once before replacing it.\n- If edit_file finds no match or several, print the exact region and retry with a smaller unique snippet. Never repeat an identical failed edit.\n\n# WHEN IN DOUBT\n- Unsure which variant the tests use: change all of them the same way.\n- Never conclude that nothing needs to change. Every task needs a non-empty source patch.\n\n# REPOSITORY EVIDENCE (found before you started; it stays here for the whole task)\nLines copied from a search of this repository for names in the issue: where they are defined, tests that use them, and lines that earlier commits changed around them. The issue stays the specification.\n{evidence?}\n', 'prompts/evidence.md': "Your only job is one search of the repository for the agent who fixes the issue after you. Your reply is copied into that agent's instructions and stays in front of it for the whole task, so it must hold real lines from the repository, not your opinions. You will not read code and you will not work on the fix.\n\nChoose two to four exact names from the issue below that the fix most likely touches: functions, methods, classes, options or parameters, written exactly as they appear in the issue or as they would appear in the code. Prefer the most specific names, such as convert_underscores, over common words, such as Header.\n\nCall run_skill_script with skill_name evidence, file_path scripts/evidence.py and positional_args holding those names, one name per item. That is your only call.\n\nThen reply with the lines of its result that best show where the fix belongs and how this code is meant to behave: where the names are defined, tests that use them, and the lines an earlier commit changed around them. Copy each line exactly as it was printed, with its file:line or commit, at most 10 lines and nothing else. If nothing in the result bears on the issue, reply with the single line NOTHING RELEVANT.\n\n# THE ISSUE (verbatim)\n{problem_description}", 'prompts/feature_fixer.md': 'You are an autonomous software engineer who adds features. The issue in the task asks for something the code does not do yet. Make the smallest complete change to the SOURCE code in /workspace so that the hidden tests for the issue pass. The hidden tests were written from the issue text, so the exact names, parameters, defaults, messages, types and behaviours in it are the specification: tests will call the new thing by exactly the name the issue uses.\n\n# RULES THAT ALWAYS HOLD\n- Your tool calls and your time are limited. When either runs out, the working tree is graded as it stands: an edit that exists counts, even without `submit_patch`, and an unedited tree scores zero.\n- Never create, modify or delete test files (`test_*.py`, `*_test.py`, anything under `tests/`, `pytest.ini`, `conftest.py`). That is an automatic failure.\n- Scratch files go in /tmp only, written with run_command. Any file left under /workspace becomes part of the graded patch.\n- Never run a whole test suite. Name one test file, and add `-k` when you can.\n- Never look outside /workspace. Every dependency is already installed.\n\n# HOW A FEATURE GETS ADDED HERE\n1. Pin down the request (no tool call): the exact name of the new option, parameter, method or class; where it is used (a function signature, a constructor, a CLI flag, a setting); its default; the behaviour with and without it; every message and type the issue gives.\n2. Find the closest existing sibling: an option or parameter of the same kind that already works the way the new one should. Start from the repository evidence at the end; search with `git grep -n -F` for the sibling and for the place the new name must appear, a few searches in one run_command, each kept to about twenty lines.\n3. Trace the sibling end to end and note every place it touches: the signature and its default, the docstring, validation, where it is stored, where it is read and acted on, and the registration points (`__all__`, option lists, dispatch tables, imports, re-exports in `__init__.py`). Also note parallel implementations (sync and async variants, typing overloads, several classes that share the API): the hidden tests often call more than one.\n4. Add the new feature along the same path, place by place, copying the sibling\'s pattern and style. Start editing as soon as you know the first place; a partial feature can be completed, an unedited tree scores zero. Keep existing signatures and defaults working.\n5. Verify: call the new API once from a short /tmp script and check the behaviour the issue describes; then run the closest existing test file, `timeout 150 python -m pytest tests/path/test_x.py -x -q -k keyword`. If a related test fails because of your change, fix the source once and rerun once.\n6. Finish: `git status --short && git diff` in one call; check that every place from step 3 got its part; remove scratch files under /workspace and restore any changed test file with `git checkout --`; call `submit_patch` and confirm that patch_size is above zero; end with one sentence.\n\n# EDIT MECHANICS\n- edit_file: old_string copied character for character from lines you printed (same indentation, no line numbers), a short unique match. write_file only for a genuinely new source file the issue requires.\n- If a tool result says "mandatory input parameters are not present", your call was cut apart because a text ended with a backtick or a stray quote. Do not send the same call again; end every text right after its last character. If it fails twice, make the change with one Python script in a quoted heredoc that asserts the old text occurs exactly once before replacing it.\n- If edit_file finds no match or several, print the exact region and retry with a smaller unique snippet. Never repeat an identical failed edit.\n\n# WHEN IN DOUBT\n- Unsure how the new thing should behave: do what the closest sibling does, under the name the issue gives.\n- Never conclude that nothing needs to change. Every task needs a non-empty source patch.\n\n# REPOSITORY EVIDENCE (found before you started; it stays here for the whole task)\nLines copied from a search of this repository for names in the issue: where they are defined, tests that use them, and lines that earlier commits changed around them. The issue stays the specification.\n{evidence?}\n', 'prompts/router.md': "Your only job is to hand the issue below to the right specialist. You do not read or change code.\n\nThe specialists:\n- docs_fixer: the change belongs in documentation code: FastAPI's `docs_src/` example programs, tutorials, or other example code that the docs show and the tests import.\n- feature_fixer: the issue asks for something the code does not do yet: a new option, parameter, argument, method, class, setting, output format, or support for a new kind of input.\n- bug_fixer: something the code already does is wrong: an exception, a crash, a wrong result, wrong output or rendering, a regression, or a missing check.\n\nWhen the issue could be read as a bug or as a feature, choose bug_fixer. When it changes behaviour that the docs example programs show, choose docs_fixer.\n\nMake exactly one call: transfer_to_agent with agent_name set to bug_fixer, feature_fixer or docs_fixer.\n\n# THE ISSUE (verbatim)\n{problem_description}\n", 'router.yaml': 'name: router\nmodel: gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct\ndescription: "Hands the issue to the right specialist."\ninstruction: !include prompts/router.md\ninclude_contents: none\nsub_agents:\n  - config_path: bug_fixer.yaml\n  - config_path: feature_fixer.yaml\n  - config_path: docs_fixer.yaml\ngenerate_content_config: !include configs/evidence_sampling.yaml\n', 'skills/evidence/SKILL.md': "---\nname: evidence\ndescription: Searches the repository's tests, definitions, git history and changelog for a few exact names from the issue. Call run_skill_script with skill_name evidence, file_path scripts/evidence.py and positional_args holding the names.\n---\n\nCall run_skill_script with skill_name evidence, file_path scripts/evidence.py and positional_args holding two to four exact\nnames from the issue, one name per item. It prints what the repository says about each name and takes a few seconds.\n", 'skills/evidence/scripts/evidence.py': '"""Repository evidence for a few exact names from the issue, in one call (arm evid1, scripts/make_mine_arms.py).\n\nRun once per task by the evidence agent through run_skill_script (skill "evidence"), names as positional_args:\n\n    run_skill_script skill_name=evidence file_path=scripts/evidence.py positional_args=["convert_underscores", "Header"]\n\nWhy: r60\'s scouts found good evidence (the right test files, the earlier commits on the buggy lines) but explored for\n2-4 minutes. This script does the same fixed searches in seconds, read-only, inside the repository:\n  - tests that mention the name (git grep in test files),\n  - where the name is defined (def / class lines),\n  - the most recent commits that added or removed the name (git log -S) and the changed lines around it in the newest one,\n  - changelog lines that mention it.\nEvery git call has its own timeout and the whole script stops adding sections after DEADLINE seconds.\n"""\nimport inspect\nimport os\nimport re\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport time\n\nONCE = True  # evid2 (r64): True -> a second call is refused (r63: told to make one call, the agent made 2-17)\nDONE_FILE = os.path.join(".git", "agent", "evidence_done")  # holds the first result, shown again on a refused call\nREPLY = \'Now reply with at most 10 lines of this result, copied exactly, and stop.\'  # evid3 (r65) swaps this: the stage\'s reply becomes its output\nDEADLINE = 8  # r76: seconds for all sections (evid3: 40; large private repos made it the stage\'s main cost)\nCALL_TIMEOUT = 4  # seconds per git call (evid3: 12)\nMAX_NAMES = 5\nOUT_MAX = 4800  # characters; the harness shows about 5000\nTEST_LINES = 6\nDEF_LINES = 3\nCOMMITS = 5\nHUNK_TRIES = 5  # changed lines come from the newest of the first HUNK_TRIES commits that has some\nHUNK_LINES = 18\nWINDOW = 3  # context lines around each changed line that has the name\nLOG_LINES = 3\nLINE_MAX = 150\nTEST_PATHS = [":(glob)**/test/**/*.py", ":(glob)**/tests/**/*.py", ":(glob)**/testing/**/*.py", ":(glob)**/test_*.py", ":(glob)**/*_test.py"]\nPY = [":(glob)**/*.py"]  # history and changed lines: Python files only (docs translations drown everything else)\nLOG_PATHS = [":(glob)**/CHANGELOG*", ":(glob)**/CHANGES*", ":(glob)**/HISTORY*", ":(glob)**/NEWS*", ":(glob)**/release-notes*",\n             ":(glob)**/changelog*"]\n\n\ndef workspace():\n    """The repository root. The skill wrapper chdirs into a temp dir, so look for the directory it started in."""\n    for frame in inspect.stack():\n        cwd = frame.frame.f_locals.get("_orig_cwd")\n        if isinstance(cwd, str) and os.path.isdir(os.path.join(cwd, ".git")):\n            return cwd\n    for cand in (os.environ.get("PYTHONPATH", "").split(os.pathsep)[0], "/workspace", os.getcwd()):\n        if cand and os.path.isdir(os.path.join(cand, ".git")):\n            return cand\n    sys.exit("repository not found")\n\n\ndef git(ws, args, start):\n    """stdout lines of one git call, [] on error, timeout or when the deadline has passed."""\n    left = DEADLINE - (time.time() - start)\n    if left < 1:\n        return None\n    try:\n        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-c", "core.quotepath=off"] + args, cwd=ws, capture_output=True, text=True,\n                           timeout=min(CALL_TIMEOUT, left), stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, errors="replace")\n    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:\n        return []\n    return r.stdout.splitlines() if r.returncode in (0, 1) else []\n\n\ndef short(line):\n    line = line.rstrip()\n    return line if len(line) <= LINE_MAX else line[:LINE_MAX] + " ..."\n\n\ndef names_from_args():\n    raw = [a for a in sys.argv[1:] if a != "--"]\n    names = []\n    for a in raw:\n        for part in re.split(r"[\\n,]", a):\n            n = part.strip().strip("`\'\\"").strip()\n            if len(n) >= 3 and n not in names:\n                names.append(n)\n    return names[:MAX_NAMES]\n\n\ndef newest_hunks(ws, commit, name, start):\n    """Changed lines that contain NAME in COMMIT (Python source, not tests), with WINDOW lines around each, at most\n    HUNK_LINES. Files the commit created or deleted are skipped: a whole new file says nothing about the change."""\n    lines = git(ws, ["show", "--format=", "-U3", "--no-color", commit, "--"] + PY\n                + [":(exclude,glob)" + p[len(":(glob)"):] for p in TEST_PATHS], start)\n    if not lines:\n        return []\n    out, cur_file, hunk, skip = [], None, [], False\n\n    def flush():\n        if skip or not hunk:\n            return\n        hits = [i for i, h in enumerate(hunk) if h[:1] in "+-" and name in h]\n        keep = sorted({j for i in hits for j in range(max(0, i - WINDOW), min(len(hunk), i + WINDOW + 1))})\n        if keep:\n            out.append(f"  {cur_file}:")\n            prev = None\n            for j in keep:\n                if prev is not None and j != prev + 1:\n                    out.append("    ...")\n                out.append("    " + short(hunk[j]))\n                prev = j\n\n    for ln in lines:\n        if ln.startswith("diff --git"):\n            flush()\n            hunk, skip, cur_file = [], False, None\n        elif ln.startswith(("new file", "deleted file")):\n            skip = True\n        elif ln.startswith("+++ "):\n            cur_file = ln[6:] if ln.startswith("+++ b/") else ln[4:]\n        elif ln.startswith(("index ", "--- ", "similarity", "rename ")):\n            continue\n        elif ln.startswith("@@"):\n            flush()\n            hunk = []\n        else:\n            hunk.append(ln)\n    flush()\n    return out[:HUNK_LINES]\n\n\n_RANGE = []\n\n\ndef recent(ws, start):\n    """r76: git log -S only over the last 500 commits (the whole history is slow on large repositories)."""\n    if not _RANGE:\n        revs = git(ws, ["rev-list", "--max-count=500", "HEAD"], start) or []\n        _RANGE.append([revs[-1] + "..HEAD"] if len(revs) == 500 else [])\n    return _RANGE[0]\n\n\ndef section(ws, name, start):\n    out = [f"== {name}"]\n    tests = git(ws, ["grep", "-n", "-I", "-F", "-e", name, "--"] + TEST_PATHS, start)\n    if tests is None:\n        return out + ["  (skipped: time)"]\n    out.append(f"tests mentioning it: {len(tests)} lines" + ("" if tests else " (none)"))\n    out.extend("  " + short(t) for t in tests[:TEST_LINES])\n    if re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*", name):\n        defs = git(ws, ["grep", "-n", "-I", "-E", "-e", rf"^[[:space:]]*(async def|def|class) {name}([^A-Za-z0-9_]|$)", "--", "*.py"], start) or []\n        if defs:\n            out.append("defined at:")\n            out.extend("  " + short(d) for d in defs[:DEF_LINES])\n    log = git(ws, ["log", "-S", name, "--no-merges", "--format=%h %ad %s", "--date=short", f"-n{COMMITS}"] + recent(ws, start) + ["--"] + PY, start)\n    if log is None:\n        return out + ["  (history skipped: time)"]\n    if log:\n        out.append("commits that added or removed it (newest first):")\n        out.extend("  " + short(c) for c in log)\n        for c in log[:HUNK_TRIES]:\n            hunks = newest_hunks(ws, c.split()[0], name, start)\n            if hunks:\n                out.append(f"what {c.split()[0]} changed around it:")\n                out.extend(hunks)\n                break\n    else:\n        out.append("commits that added or removed it: none found")\n    notes = git(ws, ["grep", "-n", "-I", "-i", "-F", "-e", name, "--"] + LOG_PATHS, start) or []\n    if notes:\n        out.append("changelog:")\n        out.extend("  " + short(n) for n in notes[:LOG_LINES])\n    return out\n\n\ndef main():\n    start = time.time()\n    names = names_from_args()\n    if not names:\n        print("NO NAMES: pass 2 to 5 exact names from the issue as positional_args, for example "\n              "[\\"function_name\\", \\"ClassName\\", \\"option_name\\"].")\n        return\n    ws = workspace()\n    done = os.path.join(ws, DONE_FILE)\n    if ONCE and os.path.exists(done):  # also after the harness re-runs the stage with no history: show the result again\n        print("ALREADY DONE: the search already ran and a second call is refused. Its result was:\\n"\n              + open(done, errors="replace").read() + "\\n" + REPLY)\n        return\n    parts = [f"REPOSITORY EVIDENCE for {\', \'.join(names)} (read-only searches; the issue stays the specification)"]\n    for n in names:\n        parts.extend(section(ws, n, start))\n    text = "\\n".join(parts)\n    if len(text) > OUT_MAX:\n        text = text[:OUT_MAX] + "\\n[cut]"\n    if ONCE:\n        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(done), exist_ok=True)\n        open(done, "w").write(text)\n        text += "\\nThis was the only search: a second call is refused. " + REPLY\n    print(text + f"\\n[done in {time.time() - start:.0f} s]")\n\n\nif __name__ == "__main__":\n    main()\n'}}
